North Korea has been under UN sanctions since 2006 over its ballistic missile and nuclear tests. But there is no solution with Pyongyang, as China and Russia often veto to protect North Korea. 5 more power development projects approved by the Royal Government
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia has decided to invest in 5 more hydropower development projects with a total capacity of 520 megawatts. The decision was made at a cabinet meeting chaired by Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen this morning, Friday, April 7, 2023.
The five power investment projects that the Royal Government has decided to invest in are: 1: Stung Russey Chrum Kandal Hydropower Project and Stung Veay Thmor Kambot Hydropower Project (Combined), 2: 4 solar power station projects (at Pursat Province 1 project, Kampong Chhnang Province 1 project, Svay Rieng Province 1 project and Prey Veng Province 1 project).
Mr. Phay Siphan, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Chairman of the Royal Government Spokesperson's Office, said that these five investment projects will enable Cambodia to get electricity from clean energy sources and reduce the use of carbon dioxide. And ensure the preservation of the environment, natural resources and climate change.
These five projects also help ensure a stable electricity tariff of 20 years for solar projects and 40 years for hydropower projects, bringing new technologies to provide jobs for people during construction and operation.
In addition, timber on the basis of the tax law in force, these five investment projects will help the Royal Government to receive a total tax revenue of approximately 322,880,382 US dollars during the business period of the five projects. Excludes other income taxes, such as payroll taxes, withholding taxes and other indirect taxes, as well as other taxes that EDC and other distributors still have to pay.
The development of power sources is a task that the Royal Government led by the Cambodian People's Party pays high attention to meet the needs of the people and to meet the needs of industry, tourism, agriculture, as well as other sectors.
According to a report by the Ministry of Mines and Energy, by 2022, Cambodia will have a total of 4,246.77 megawatts of electricity. Of that, 3,116.42 megawatts, equivalent to more than 73 percent of the electricity generated from domestic development, and 1,130.75 megawatts, equivalent to more than 26 percent of the electricity imported from neighboring countries.
