Mr. John Wallis made this remark when he was invited to represent the Wildlife Alliance at a press conference on the second phase of the Zero Trap in Pursat province. The ceremony was presided over by Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment and Chairman of the Zero Trap Campaign on the afternoon of April 6, 2023.
He said that the decision by the Wildlife Alliance to participate in the second Zero Trap campaign, organized by the Ministry of Environment and partner organizations, was due to the impact of trapping on all types of wildlife in the protected area. Cambodia.
For John Wellis, "People and conservationists have likened traps to landmines because traps kill indiscriminately, traps also destroy the jobs and economic opportunities of innocent people for the campaign." The Zero Trap, led by the Ministry of Environment, is an important campaign to protect Cambodia's economic development as well as wildlife.
In addition, he said, in 2022, officials from the Wildlife Alliance and park officials removed more than 10,000 meters of nets and more than 50,000 mixed traps. In 2022, officials also rescued more than 400 species of wildlife, most of which were trapped.
"Based on the protection of the Cardamom Mountains, the Wildlife Alliance believes that frequent wildlife threats could also affect Cambodia's economic development, and that trapping hunters can benefit in the short term, but others "The other innocent in the community is lost." This is Mr. Wallis's additional confirmation.
John Wellis said the trap is affecting revenue through the sale of carbon credits, as well as tourist revenue. Companies that buy carb loans are always interested in projects that protect wildlife. In addition, the sale of carbon credits in the Cardamom Mountains is funded by the construction of water wells, roads, schools, health centers, toilets, especially to support the scholarship fund in Koh Kong since 2019.
"The selfish actions of these trappers are hurting the people of Slovenia, the wildlife that has lost customers who have purchased carbon credits and will turn to other countries, not countries," he said. No Cambodia. ”
According to Mr. Net Pheaktra, Pursat province is rich in wildlife, which is necessary for all stakeholders and people to participate in the protection and conservation for future generations and to participate in the promotion of ecotourism.
He said the Zero Trap campaign was aimed at raising public awareness of the consequences of trapping wildlife, wildlife trafficking and wildlife consumption, including risks to public health and loss of natural resources. Also. The campaign will also continue to spread the word about Cambodia's need for wildlife conservation and Cambodia's rich natural resources to support sustainable development.
