The banking risk "may increase in the coming months amid continued tightening monetary policy," the AFP news agency quoted a group of IMF economists as saying in a blog post. "Global" and spread to the interconnected non-banking sector, which now accounts for almost half of global financial assets.
This post is part of our special coverage Global Financial Stability Report.
Non-bank financial intermediaries (NBFIs), such as pension institutions and investment funds, have risen sharply since the 2008 global financial crisis, when regulators tightened rules, AFP reported. On the bank.
IMF economists write that the large size of the NBFI sector means that "the smooth running of the non-banking sector is essential for financial stability." To address the issue properly, policymakers must use a variety of tools, including the adoption of stronger sector oversight and regulation, and forcing companies to share more data about the risks they face, the IMF said. They are receiving.
The central bank also has a role to play in doing this, focusing on targeted support for non-bank financial intermediaries (NBFIs) that pose a risk to financial stability and for any support that is considered Systematically important. According to the AFP news agency.
