Such a push by Mr. Suon Serey Ratha was made when he invited Dr. Ouk Rabun, Minister of Rural Development, to organize a workshop on disseminating national policies for the development of indigenous peoples in Cambodia, organized by Department of Indigenous Development of the Ministry of Rural Development this morning, April 6, 2023 at Tonle Bassac 2 Restaurant.
Participants: Representatives of the Ministry of Interior, Ministry of Land, Ministry of Agriculture, Ministry of Environment, Ministry of Cult, Ministry of Culture, Ministry of Education, Ministry of Health, Deputy Provincial Governors, representatives of 15 provincial governors, 42 district governors living with indigenous peoples, and the Department of Rural Development. All 15 provinces and officials of the Ministry of Rural Development, a total of 110 people.
The purpose of this workshop is:
First, increase awareness and capacity building for all sub-national officials on laws, national policies and legal standards in the process of identifying indigenous peoples.
Second: Disseminate the roles and responsibilities between the national and sub-national levels
And 3: Increase good cooperation between national, sub-national government institutions and partner organizations in the process of conservation and development of indigenous peoples in Cambodia in accordance with the national policy and the Rectangular Strategy Phase 4 of the Royal Government.
On that occasion, Mr. Suon Serey Ratha emphasized that the relevant inter-ministerial institutions would like to examine the possibility of working together to address and provide services to indigenous peoples in Cambodia equally as the general population living under the umbrella of the Constitution. One Royal Government, and especially under the umbrella of the peace of the high leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia.
Tomorrow morning, the Ministry of Environment and partner organizations will launch the second Zero Trap Campaign in Pursat Province with the aim of reducing the number of traps and cultivating wildlife.
(Pursat): In an effort to reduce and prevent all kinds of traps in protected areas, officials of the Ministry of Environment, civil society organizations and journalists, as well as Pursat Provincial Administration and relevant departments will launch a zero trapping campaign in Pursat on April 7, 2023
The main purpose of this campaign is to reduce and prevent the number of traps in protected areas in Pursat province, as well as in protected areas in Cambodia, and to cultivate a greater sense of wildlife among the public.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment said that Pursat is the first province to launch the second phase of the zero trapping campaign.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra has 6 nature reserves: South Cardamom Mountain National Park, Central Cardamom Mountain National Park, Phnom Samkos Wildlife Sanctuary, Oral Mountain Wildlife Sanctuary, Tonle Sap Lake Multi-use Area and Biodiversity Conservation Corridor. Nature protection covers a total area of 689,297 hectares.
In addition, the senior official of the Ministry of Environment said that according to the results of the study of officials of the Ministry of Environment and partner organizations in Pursat province, the abundance of wildlife that all stakeholders and citizens must participate in the protection and conservation. Survive for generations to come and be involved in promoting ecotourism connecting the land.
(Svay Rieng): A fire broke out in two houses and turned to ashes, causing a man to die at 3:00 AM on April 6, 2023 in Sandor village, Chrey Thom commune. Romeas Hek District, Svay Rieng Province.
According to the report from Romeas Hek District Authority, under the appointment of Mr. Men Vibol, Chairman of the Svay Rieng Provincial Branch of the Cambodian Red Cross on April 6, 2023, the Provincial Red Cross Branch Team led by Mr. Pov Saren, Deputy Director The branch and its colleagues brought humanitarian donations to the families of the victims, 50-year-old Pich Saban and his wife, Ken Sun, 57, in Sandor village, Chrey Thom commune, Romeas Hek district, Svay Rieng province.
Authorities said Pich Sa was killed and two houses were damaged in the incident, with tiled roofs and concrete roofs, as well as galvanized iron roofs and leaf walls, as well as a large amount of property.
The Royal Government and development partners hold a meeting to collect input to prepare a new socio-economic policy for 5 years
The dialogue was chaired by Mr. Vongsey Visoth, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister, Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, and was attended by development partners and stakeholders. The event was held at GARDEN CITY Hotel, Phnom Penh.
Speaking on the occasion, Mr. Vongsey Visoth also encouraged all participants to exchange opinions and actively participate in this dialogue, he said that the Ministry is preparing a new socio-economic agenda for the Royal Government in the next 5 years. From 2024 to 2028.
Mr. Vongsey Visoth said that the Royal Government has encouraged the participation of all stakeholders to seek opinions as well as to ensure the environment and ownership of the activities carried out together in the spirit of the Royal Government. Implement to ensure joint efforts to achieve goals for the people of Cambodia.
"We can take a number of measures and we need to focus on key areas to better prepare Cambodia for a competitive environment," he said. "Now there is a lot of uncertainty that does not know where to go, but to build resilience to ensure better results for Cambodia to reduce the negative impact and seize the opportunity to pull off this change."
Mr. Vongsey Visoth stated that the results of this dialogue will provide input to the relevant ministries and the Royal Government to formulate sectoral policies and strategies of the Royal Government in the next five years.
Mr. Vongsey Visoth said that 2022 is a difficult year, not only for Cambodia but also for the whole world.
He said the spread of Kovid-19 has slowed economic growth and reduced Cambodia's ability to become a high-middle-income country by 2030, as Cambodia returns to high-growth economy. Many challenges that can affect long-term growth.
In addition, he said, economic diversification and private sector participation are still limited, as human resources to meet the skills needs for the digital economy are needed to accelerate development. Lack of infrastructure and transport services also limits the Royal Government's capacity, and Cambodia is vulnerable to climate change disasters.
He said Cambodia was in a regional climate and that policymakers needed to put in place new strategies to accelerate growth and at the same time find ways to mitigate the last three years, as well as the challenges that lie ahead. Continue to occur due to geopolitical tensions in the world to make Cambodia green.
