(Phnom Penh): A Singaporean agricultural company that is investing in cassava and flour processing is looking for a partner to grow cassava to supply domestic and foreign markets as cassava orders are on the rise.
LHH Engineering Co., Ltd. (Cambodia) Limited and Hong Lai Huot Group Limited, a Singaporean company, have been investing in Cambodia for many years. The company has established the Cambodia-Singapore Agricultural Center (CSAH) on an area of about 10,000 hectares in Kampong Speu province's Oral district to concentrate investment in agriculture, such as food processing, animal husbandry, commercial space and solar plantations.
LHH Agricultural Co., Ltd. (Cambodia) Limited and Hong Lai Huot Group Limited have been investing in maize and cassava cultivation and processing for export to the above location since 2008 after receiving the investment rights. From the Royal Government.
Currently, the company has been cultivating cassava and processing it into cassava, exporting it to foreign markets and supplying it locally. Due to the increase in overseas orders for cassava and domestic demand, the company has explored the possibility of expanding cassava cultivation on the vast area where it has received investment rights from the Royal Government.
The company has announced that it will acquire up to 5,000 hectares in the first phase in 2023 for joint cooperation and strategic partnership to grow cassava and process it in its existing factories.
The company hopes that this initiative will attract as well as welcome investors in the agricultural sector, subcontractors, farmers and other experts from all over Cambodia to join the company in discussing a partnership with the company to expand the company's existing cassava plantations. .
The company welcomes all kinds of agricultural crops that will be grown under the first phase with cassava, followed by mangoes, bananas, sugar cane, cashews and many other crops. Under this partnership model, the company will provide land, and the participating contractors will prepare the land for weeding and harvesting.
If the contractor chooses to grow cassava on the allotted land, the company will provide a repurchase guarantee option in accordance with the terms of the cooperation agreement between the company and the contractor. The company also operates an on-site cassava mill that can produce 120 tonnes of cassava and needs up to 500 tonnes of cassava for daily production.
The company also plans to set up a cassava collection center on site to collect cassava from nearby farmers. The company has now built two large warehouses on site that can hold about 20,000 tonnes of cassava per day. With the successful implementation of this project, the supply of cassava has become more stable and can create opportunities for investors to set up new cassava flour mills on site.
The company hopes that this initiative will contribute to the growth of Cambodia's agricultural sector, especially on food security and in line with the Royal Government's policy of support for Cambodia's agricultural sector.
If you are interested in this investment, please contact Ms. Tayfa at 098 297 999 and 078 707 727 to find out more and to arrange a visit in person. Phnom Penh Governor Raises New Flag at Wat Phnom Resort for Happy Khmer New Year
