Malaysia, China sign agreement to stabilize palm oil supply chain
KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysia said on Sunday that it had signed a memorandum of understanding with a Chinese government trade association to boost palm oil trade and cooperation. According to Reuters.
Reuters, quoting the Malaysian Palm Oil Council, saying its partnership with the Chinese Chamber of Commerce for the import and export of food, raw materials and animal products would help Malaysia regain market share in the country. With the largest population in the world.
Malaysia, the world's second-largest producer of palm oil, and China, the world's second-largest buyer, will jointly promote Malaysia's sustainable use of palm oil in China, as well as Develop and implement new technologies such as artificial intelligence in oil palm plantations.
"China intends to work with Malaysia to ensure the supply of palm oil to the country," the Malaysian Palm Oil Council said in a statement. The council added that the memorandum "will help boost China's confidence in Malaysia's palm oil."
The Malaysian Palm Oil Council said the agreement "will facilitate China's participation in the exploration of technology for palm oil mechanisms in Malaysia, which will increase productivity and reduce dependence on human labor in Malaysia." In the fields.
In 2022, Malaysia exported 3.14 million tonnes of palm oil and palm oil products to China, making China its most important trading partner for edible oil after India. According to Reuters.
