Cambodia and Australia launch new fragrant rice variety Champis 70
(Phnom Penh): 70 Champis rice varieties are officially present after spending 9 years researching and experimenting on 80 farmers' fields. This is according to a joint press release from the Ministry of Agriculture and the Australian Embassy on April 5.
Champey 70 is bred from Cambodia's Phkar Rumduol rice variety, which is the best award-winning rice in the world with CNi9024 non-season rice. This rice variety has been tested on 80 farmers in both the dry and wet seasons.
Three field days were organized with the participation of about 90 farmers, one-third of whom were women, to evaluate the best threads for breeding. A thread is selected and submitted to the Plant Breeding Commission of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries.
Champis 70 is a fragrant rice variety that takes only 3 months to grow. This rice is a non-seasonal rice variety and resistant to climate change, floods and drought. Farmers can grow 70 Champis seeds two to three times a year, leading to increased production and yield. This new variety will also significantly reduce the capital burden for planting, storage costs and post-harvest losses.
Agriculture is an important pillar of the Cambodian economy, contributing nearly a quarter of Cambodia's GDP in 2021. The sector employs more than a third of Cambodia's total, with more than half of all workers being women.
Aromatic rice is in high demand worldwide, accounting for more than 60 percent of Cambodia's total rice exports. The Cambodian Rice Federation has set a target to export 1 million tons of rice by 2025.
Mr. Dith Tina, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, said that "Champis 70 was named by the Prime Minister of Cambodia Samdech Techo Hun Sen to celebrate the 70th anniversary of important diplomatic relations between Cambodia and Australia. . "This new rice variety will significantly contribute to the diversification of fragrant rice production and export of fragrant rice to international markets, which will improve the lives of Cambodian farmers."
