Mine Authority leads the meeting of the Joint Technical Working Group of the Royal Government, Development Partners on Mine Action, in 2023, Cambodia plans to clear mines in an area of 411 square kilometers.
(Phnom Penh): On April 4, 2023, on behalf of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia and Chairman of the Mine Authority, Senior Minister Ly Thuch, First Vice President of the Mine Authority, chaired a meeting of the Joint Technical Working Group of the Royal Government and Development Partners on Mine action that took place at the Residence Sokha Hotel, Phnom Penh.
Senior Minister Ly Thuch said that the 30-year humanitarian mine action has not only contributed to the maintenance of peace and security, but also contributed to the reduction of poverty and development in Cambodia through the safe use of land. Of the 2,579 square kilometers cleared, about 76% of the land is used for agricultural activities.
He added that in the last six years, Cambodia has cleared more than a third of the landmines and explosive remnants of war cleared in 30 years. Meanwhile, in the last three years, 2020-2022, Cambodia has cleared 420 square kilometers of landmines, equivalent to half of the humanitarian demining work of the 33 States Parties of the Ottawa Convention, which is facing demining operations. .
The Senior Minister continued that despite the efforts, as of the first quarter of 2023, Cambodia continues to be affected by landmines and explosive remnants of war on an area of 1,885 square kilometers, including minefields. Of the 563 square kilometers that Cambodia is obliged to complete by 2025, 729 square kilometers are cluster munitions and 593 square kilometers are other unexploded ordnance.
He added that after 2025, Cambodia still needs more support from development partners and philanthropists in dealing with cluster munitions, other unexploded ordnance, victim rescue and awareness activities on the risk of explosive remnants of war. .
