(Phnom Penh): Mr. Srey Thamrong, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister, said that the leaders of the Mekong River will do their best to take care of the Mekong River Basin, the Mekong River water for the benefit of the people living in the Mekong River Basin, and to protect the environment. Maintaining ecological balance, learning about flood resistance, drought, sustainable use of water and Mekong River resources.
On the morning of April 4, 2023, Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen led a high-level delegation to attend the 4th Mekong River Commission Summit in Vientiane, Laos. The invitation of Samdech Techo Prime Minister was made in response to the invitation of Mr. Sorn Sai Siphandon, Prime Minister of Laos.
Speaking to reporters at Phnom Penh International Airport, Mr. Srey Thamrong said that the Mekong River leaders will also have a lot of discussions on the economic interests of the people living along the Mekong River who can benefit from the Mekong River. At the same time, due to unpredictable climate change, Mekong leaders will also share their country's measures to adapt to the climate.
According to the Cambodian Ministry of Foreign Affairs, the 4th Mekong River Commission Summit, held in Laos on April 5, will be held under the theme "Innovation and Cooperation for Water Security Basin." And sustainable. ”
The Mekong River Commission (MRC) is an intergovernmental body that works to facilitate cooperation around the development and sustainable management of the Mekong Basin.
The members of the commission include Cambodia, Laos, Thailand and Vietnam. The Mekong River Commission serves as a forum for water diplomacy and regional cooperation through which member countries share the benefits of shared water resources and respond to cross-border challenges in the basin. These member countries have different national interests, however.
The commission also serves as a knowledge center for advocating for evidence-based policy-making. The Commission provides technical assistance and develops tools to assist member countries in making informed decisions.
Member countries have agreed to cooperate in areas such as fisheries, flood management, irrigation, hydropower and navigation. China and Myanmar, at the top of the Mekong River, have been MRC dialogue partners since 1996. Lt. Gen. Lon Sophat, Representative of Lt. Gen. Neth Savoeun, Closes a Meeting to Review the Situation and Results of 2022 Operations of Kampot Provincial Police
