The Cambodian Ministry of Foreign Affairs said that the invitation of Samdech Techo Prime Minister was made in response to the invitation of Mr. Sorn Sai Siphandon, Prime Minister of Laos. So we can all take this opportunity to understand some of the history and key potential that this neighbor has:
1: General information about Laos
2: Key leaders
3: History
In the 19th century, this territory was incorporated into the French Protectorate of Indochina. Under French tutelage, Vientiane became the capital of the Lao PDR. Shortly after a Japanese occupation of World War II, Laos declared independence in 1945. Unfortunately, the French protectorate returned to occupying the territory until 1950. Laos gained full independence in 1954 under a constitutional monarchy.
During the Vietnam War, the eastern part of Laos fell under the control of the North Vietnamese army as a base for transporting weapons and food to the army to counter the South Vietnamese army. In response, the United States launched a bombing campaign against North Vietnam against the communist regime. Eventually, a series of coups ensued, and a civil war between the Lao government and the communist movement began.
4: Geographical and climatic conditions
The Lao economy is heavily dependent on investment and trade with neighboring countries. Previously, the country did not have much modern infrastructure, but in recent years, due to friendly and economic ties with China, Laos inaugurated the first high-speed railway in 2021, while the main roads are also Start developing as well.
However, in remote rural areas, electricity, internet and other public services are still struggling. Half of the agricultural products are consumed by 80% of the population. Laos has the smallest permanent cultivated area in the Greater Mekong Subregion. In fact, the cultivated area is only 4.01%, of which the permanent cultivated area is only 0.34%, with rice being the most important crop.
The Lao economy is supported by the International Monetary Fund and other international sources, as well as foreign investment. Tourism is the fastest growing industry in Laos. On November 24, 2021, the United Nations General Assembly (UNGA) recognized that Laos has risen from the status of a Least Developed Country (LDC).
