General knowledge: The deadliest earthquake in history
According to the descriptions in those records, the Chea Jing earthquake seemed to be very different from other earthquakes. Records claim that mountains collapsed, floods and fires raged for days, while the landscape changed dramatically. The same record estimates that some areas are likely to have lost up to 60 percent of their population.
Although we can not be 100% sure that the estimates are accurate, the Jingjing earthquake can still be considered the deadliest earthquake ever to date due to its magnitude. The death toll is far higher than in any other earthquake disaster.
The deadliest earthquake in the world was the 2004 tsunami, which triggered a 2004 tsunami in the Indian Ocean. It has killed 230,000 people in Indonesia, Thailand, Sri Lanka, India and other affected countries.
After the Richter scale was developed in the 1930s, scientists concluded that the quake was probably 8 to 8.3 on the Richter scale. Although it is not the strongest level in history, it is certainly an enormous level.
