Mr. Som Sovann, Director of Business Department of Phnom Penh Water Supply Authority, said that for the past 7 days, the Water Supply Authority has been delivering water to supply people who are experiencing water shortage by installing 5,000-liter water tanks in 17 locations. Where there is a shortage of water for most people to use to meet daily needs such as cooking, bathing and laundry.
He said that the Water Supply Authority will continue to supply water to people who lack water until the first phase of the Bak Kheng water treatment plant in May.
Hay Sok Khim, chief of Samrong Krom commune, said that the commune had been without water for a month. Out of 11 villages, 6 villages were completely flooded. Authorities, in collaboration with the Water Supply Authority and private companies, have set up six distribution bins in the district for residents to use.
Mr. Chea Sovann, Chief of Prey Popel Village, Sangkat Samrong Krom, Khan Por Sen Chey, said that since March 26, the Phnom Penh Water Supply Authority has been supplying water to the people, which makes the people in this village have temporary water supply. When the factory broke down. He requested the Water Supply Authority to continue to deliver water to the people until May until the water in the network is used normally again.
Heang Pov, 56, from Doung Ta On village, Samrong Krom commune, Por Sen Chey district, who came to fetch water from the PPWSA's distribution tank, showed some difficulties after her family faced a water shortage half a month ago. Once a day, she has to bring water from a distribution tank, which is about 4 to 30 liters for use, such as cooking, eating and bathing for children.
She thanked the Phnom Penh Water Supply Authority for delivering clean water to the people in this area and requested to be distributed every day so that the people have water to use.
