Official launch of a research project on the use of innovative technologies for improving maternal and child health in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): Under the joint collaboration between the University of Health Sciences and the National University of Singapore on March 31, 2023, it was decided to officially launch a research project using innovative technology (i-MoMCARE) for maternal health and Babies in Cambodia.
Professor Saphon Vattanak said that under the wise leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Cambodia has been peaceful and developed in all areas.
In that, health services have also achieved many great successes. Achieve the Millennium Development Goals to reduce maternal, infant and child mortality, reduce morbidity and mortality with infectious diseases, with national and international recognition, especially with the United Nations and UN agencies. World Health.
"Along with this progress, we also face a number of challenges, such as maternal and child health checks after the second birth has not reached the target, referrals and follow-up cases of malnutrition between health centers," he said. And the community is not doing well yet.
"I sincerely hope that through the official launch of this research project, the research team and partners will be able to find more important inputs for health policy planning," he said. "Mothers and babies to contribute to the better implementation of the national health policy in Cambodia."
She added that the project will help provide services to women and children faster because it has created an app for the district health department and health center, especially the volunteers in the village to communicate and provide information to each other. Return quickly when a woman or child has a medical condition.
Dr. Kim Ratana, President of the National Center for Maternal and Child Health, said that the i-MoMCARE project is a new initiative for intervention to encourage women and children to receive timely check-ups and treatment, especially with the participation of the authorities. The base has a village health support group as a pillar.
