Samdech Prime Minister: People should not be too afraid of the warning of the global economic downturn until they withdraw money from the bank (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia this morning On March 30, 2023, major global financial institutions such as the World Monetary Fund and the World Bank issued warnings of a global economic downturn.
Despite warnings of the risk of a global economic downturn, Hun Sen continued to call on people not to panic and withdraw money from banks.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen expects that the world's largest economies will find ways to manage their economies so that they do not fall further.
He added that the economic downturn due to the Kovid 19 crisis was one thing, but the unilateral sanctions combined with the war that started between Russia and Ukraine created a dire situation for the global economy.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen's remarks were made on the occasion of handing over certificates and diplomas to more than 6,000 trainees and students of the National Institute of Business on the morning of March 30, 2023.
She said 2023 would be another difficult year, with global economic growth slowing to less than 3 percent due to the effects of the epidemic, the war in Ukraine and the tightening of monetary policy.
The IMF, which has projected global growth of 2.9 percent this year, is set to release a new forecast next month. The IMF is paying close attention to the most vulnerable countries, especially low-income countries with high debt levels.
The World Bank (WB) has warned that global average economic growth will slow to a three-decade low of 2.2 percent a year until 2030, a period called "one." A lost decade ”for the world economy. To avoid this complication, policymakers must adopt ambitious initiatives to boost labor supply, productivity and investment.
