Dr. Kin Phea: Democracy must be looked at beyond the political system
Democracy does not have a single set of institutions and features that are universally applicable. The specific form in which democracy takes place in a country must be broadly related to the existing political, social and economic situation in that country. It is not possible to propagate democracy from one country to another in the hope of achieving the same results.
Therefore, no country has the right to use the ideology and values of democracy as a tool to suppress or intimidate other countries and to promote its geopolitical strategies, incite divisions and confrontations on the international stage and push the world back to the modern era. Dangerous Cold War on the pretext of promoting democracy.
Causing divisions and confrontations in the name of democracy is nothing more than trampling and betraying the spirit and values of democracy. This will bring chaos and chaos to the world and destroy peace and development. This should be resisted and opposed by the international community.
The world needs to think of global public opinion and gather more energy through unity, and all peoples, nations and regions should join in this effort, focusing on building a multilateral consensus.
Democracy is the right of the people in every country, not the rights of any one country. Whether a country is democratic or not should be determined by the people of that country, because democracy comes in many different forms.
In an attempt to divert attention from its failures and domestic problems, these liberal-heritage states have shifted the blame to other countries. Take Afghanistan, Iraq, Libya, Tunisia, Somalia, Syria and Yemen as examples. These countries and their people are under attack and continue to suffer under the banner of democracy and human rights.
The distinctive features of civilization are no different from the distinctive features of democracy. The rich diversity and vibrancy of civilization is reflected in the various forms of democracy.
No country has the exclusive right to define the definition of democracy, nor can it determine the right of the people to explore and choose the model of democracy that best suits their nation.
