90% of Phnom Penh's first toilet waste treatment plant completed, with plans to inaugurate
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of March 29, 2023, the Project Management Committee was chaired by Mr. Nuon Pharath, Deputy Governor of Phnom Penh and Mr. Huot Hay and Mr. Suy Serith, Deputy Governor of Phnom Penh and Deputy Chairman of the Commission. Led a professional team to inspect the technical and progress of the construction of the toilet waste treatment station, located in Sangkat Pleung Cheh Roret, Khan Kampol, Phnom Penh.
The first phase of the toilet waste treatment plant project in Phnom Penh has achieved about 90 percent of the planned results, which will be completed by the end of April.
Mr. Nuon Pharath should note that this project to manage the toilet waste is intended to contribute to improving the well-being of the people in Phnom Penh through three main activities, including:
* First: Proper management of toilet mud, which will improve the quality of sewage in Phnom Penh, because in the past, some people have used this waste to fill the fields in the canals in Phnom Penh * Second : Creating jobs and processing toilet sludge into organic fertilizers, reducing the use of chemical fertilizers * Third: Selling organic fertilizers produced in the agricultural market.
The Phnom Penh Municipal Administration plans to officially inaugurate the station in mid-May, coinciding with the annual meeting of the city network, a member of the International Association of French-speaking Mayors (AIMF) in Southeast Asia, headquartered at the school. Phnom Penh itself.
The construction of this treatment plant is in line with the "Phnom Penh Mud Waste Management Strategy 2035" which was started in 2018 through a technical assistance from AIMF.
Observing the success of the first phase of construction, the Phnom Penh Capital Administration strongly hopes to be able to raise additional funds to build the next treatment plant as planned to contribute to the well-being of the people in Phnom Penh. General Neth Savoeun Decides to Assign Lieutenant General Prum Santhor, Deputy Commissioner General of Criminal Police
