(Phnom Penh): The National Social Security Fund (NSSF) in collaboration with Gretchen will pilot a social security scheme for healthcare for tricycle riders in Phnom Penh to expand social insurance to those working in the field. Informal economy.
The newly announced project will require drivers to pay a contribution fee of 15,600 riels per month through partner banks.
To receive benefits, registered members must pay a contribution fee for two consecutive months. The medical and healthcare services provided through this project include inpatient services at health centers accredited by the Ministry of Health, as well as maternity and emergency services.
Mr. Ouk Somvithya, the Royal Government delegate in charge of the Director General of the Socialist Republic of Cambodia, said that currently employed in the informal economy accounted for more than 65% of the Cambodian economy.
He added that the pilot project is expected to be a learning mechanism for expanding social security coverage for all self-employed individuals in Cambodia.
The pilot project is part of a joint venture between the UNFPA and Gretchen, with support from AFD to achieve an integrated social security system for those working in the informal economy, such as tricycle riders and You use housework.
Over the years, GREAT has built a strong partnership with the CCHR to pursue its goals in the social security system set by the Royal Government of Cambodia. Expanding social insurance for tricycle riders will be a step towards continuing to expand this social security scheme to all self-employed individuals in Cambodia.
