During the bilateral meeting on March 27, 2023 at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, African Cooperation and Moroccan Immigration in Raba, the Ministers of the two countries exchanged fruitful views and expressed confidence that the visit It will provide a new impetus to cooperation between the two countries through the search for cooperation and the launch of new initiatives.
Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon praised King Mohammed VI's leadership and praised Morocco's social and economic development, as well as his wise policies, which he sought to ensure. Prosperity and stability of the Kingdom and the people of Morocco.
The Ministers of the two countries commended the excellent relations that bind the two kingdoms to the highest level, which is noted through friendship, mutual respect, continuous consultation and cooperation. Coordinate various issues to serve mutual benefits.
In this regard, both sides expressed satisfaction with the holding of a video meeting at the ministerial level of the two countries on January 25, 2022 and the third political consultation meeting between Morocco-Cambodia at the General level. Director on May 18, 2022 and expressed interest in promoting bilateral political-diplomatic dialogue.
The two ministers highlighted the need for trade and investment development between the two countries and highlighted the potential for building economic cooperation based on their respective locations in North Africa and Asia. Southeast.
In this regard, the two ministers encouraged the business community of the two countries to increase trade and investment activities.
In this regard, the Ministers of the two countries have expressed their willingness to hold an important business forum in Morocco or Cambodia in the future.
The Ministers of the two countries also shared the common views between Morocco and Cambodia on a number of regional and international issues and reaffirmed their strong commitment to promoting multilateralism and contributing to the global agenda for peace, security and development. Development.
In response to Morocco's request for a Sectoral Dialogue Partnership with ASEAN, Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon reiterated to his Moroccan counterpart the full support of Cambodia for the ASEAN Secretariat re-evaluation of the Moroccan proposal in accordance with due process. And the ASEAN Consensus.
The ministers of the two countries welcomed the election and mutual support within the framework of international organizations.
Similarly, in connection with the friendly relations between the two Kingdoms, as well as Morocco's leadership in participating in international security, Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon expressed the Kingdom's support for the Kingdom of Morocco's candidacy for a non-permanent membership. United Nations Security Council for the 2028-2029 term.
The Ministers of the two countries exchanged views on ways and means to develop people-to-people contacts through scholarships, exchanges, studies, cultural events, etc.
At the end of the meeting, the Ministers of the two countries agreed on a roadmap in the near future to further deepen bilateral ties in order to build a comprehensive partnership.
The Ministers of the two countries welcomed the signing of the Air Transport Agreement between the Government of the Kingdom of Cambodia and the Government of the Kingdom of Morocco and expressed confidence that this agreement will contribute to the promotion of tourism, business and tourism. The connection between the people and the people of the two countries.
In the same spirit, the two Ministers agreed to expedite negotiations and sign three other pending agreements, including:
* Agreement on the Promotion and Protection of Mutual Investment between Morocco and Cambodia
* Agreement on the Elimination of Duplication of Taxes Related to Income Taxes and Prevention of Tax Evasion and Tax Avoidance between Morocco and Cambodia
* Agreement on cultural, technical, scientific and economic cooperation.
These three documents will give new impetus to the economy, bilateral relations and cooperation, and be a solid foundation for boosting trade and investment activities, reflecting the excellent relations that link the two countries at both the political and diplomatic levels.
Netanyahu says he is committed to approving judicial reform but wants widespread approval. And the postponement means the bill will not be put to a vote in parliament until the end of April. "When there is an opportunity to avoid civil war through dialogue, as prime minister, I am taking the time to set up a dialogue," Netanyahu said in a televised address on Monday.
The government's plan to tighten parliamentary control over the judiciary has sparked one of the biggest protests in Israeli history, with opposition groups calling it a threat to democracy. The chaos has led to many shutdowns in the country and economic disruption. Flights at Ben Gurion International Airport have been suspended, while work at major seaports has also been suspended.
