At the same time, the participants also watched some documentary videos produced by the Documentation Center of Cambodia recently related to short interviews with other survivors in the provinces of the country. Cambodia, especially Kampot, Battambang and Kampong Cham.
There was no discussion of these videos, but the conversation in the video made all the participants whisper about the recollection of the activities of the Cambodian volunteers who had interviewed him at home. The situation is similar.
Mr. Kim Sovanndani, Project Coordinator of "Promoting the Rights and Improving the Health of Khmer Rouge Survivors", began his discussion with participants regarding health and disability.
He said that after a short presentation on health care, Sovan Dany asked all participants about the type of disease and the reasons that lead to disability to this day. Through the overall survey, we observed that most of the participants had diabetes, high blood pressure, and chronic gastritis. Most of the participants had vision problems, dizziness, and blindness.
Oum Pros Onpon, 74, now living in Prey Kansa village, Cham commune, Kampong Trabek district, described the reason for his disability that he had his left leg amputated in 1960 when he was hit by a 60mm bullet. Fighting between North and South Vietnam.
"At that time, I was herding cows near the house. Occasionally there is a fight. My parents never ran away from home, despite the fighting in the village. "I was hit by a 60mm bullet and it stuck to my feet forever."
Pon's leg was recently amputated in 2020 because he could not bear the pain. After amputating his leg and recovering, he was no longer in pain, but he suffered from trauma for nearly three years due to the loss of one of his legs.
Pon recalled his anger when he was shot, saying that he volunteered for the Khmer Liberation Army and served in the Khmer Rouge until the end of 1978, before he was taken into custody. In Kampong Chhnang province. In 1974, Pon took over as the "B" commander, managing 30 soldiers.
His members were repeatedly killed during battle with Lon Nol forces. During a fight in Svay Chrum district, Svay Rieng province, Pon suffered another back injury. He was taken to hospital for treatment. After April 17, 1975, Pon had to join the same Khmer Rouge soldiers on the Cambodian-Vietnamese border.
My strength was lost during the fighting, and new forces were recruited from the village to replace the dead. One day in 1974, I was hit in the spine during a battle in the battle of Svay Chrum, Svay Rieng province. I was sent to the hospital and returned to the army after the liberation on April 17.
While sitting and listening to the discussion in the forum, Pon always touches his leg, which was amputated in 2020. His face now is happy and friendly to the team. He talked a lot about his experience through the Khmer Rouge regime. In addition to disability, there is another physical illness, high blood pressure.
Pon is not worried about his physical illness because he monitors his health regularly. "I looked at my health. Bleeding up to 17 or 18 at a time. Once down to 13 or 14. As a return message to Pon and other participants, Sovan Dany presented a booklet prepared by the Documentation Center of Cambodia on some methods of health care, especially the 10 types of chronic diseases.
Pon and the other participants expressed their happiness and joy at being invited to this morning's forum and thanked for setting up the event.
