Samdech Techo Hun Sen Instructs the Ministry of Economy to Examine Additional Capital for Cashew Nut Processing to Turn Cambodia into a Future Export Emperor (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, has instructed the Ministry of Economy and Finance Things to increase capital for investors to increase the processing of cashew nuts in the country to participate in turning Cambodia into a future cashew emperor.
This statement by Samdech Techo Hun Sen was made at the closing ceremony of the meeting to review the work of the old year and raise the work direction of the new year of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries on the afternoon of March 24, 2023.
On that occasion, Samdech Techo stated that Cambodia has the ability to compete for the number one position in the world in exporting cashew nuts. Today, Cambodia has surpassed Ivory Coast in the export of cashew nuts, while Ivory Coast has only 1,100,000 tons of cashew nuts, but Cambodia has more than 1,180,000 tons of cashew nuts.
Samdech Techo continued that currently the area under unprocessed cashew cultivation is also increasing, so if Cambodia has a good processing plant with sufficient capital, the production of cashew nuts will increase even more while cashew nuts are in demand. Lots in the world.
The Prime Minister added that Vietnam and India are the largest exporters of cashew nuts, but they import cashew nuts for processing in their respective countries, but Cambodia already has cashew nuts in the country. Processing requires research, proper and quality packaging.
"I hope that the Ministry of Economy and Finance of the National Bank will cooperate to promote this work," he said. "More investment must be made in cashew processing in this country in order to achieve the successful cashew policy we have adopted in the past to become a supplier."
Samdech Techo Hun Sen stressed that for the sale of cashew nuts, it is up to the market to decide who has the highest price to sell to them, whether in the country or abroad.
"Let the market judge the economic rules, so it is less likely that when it comes to processing, farmers are prohibited from selling to foreign countries where they come to buy in our country, not a market economy, a market economy," he said. "The market decides who sells at a higher price, does not have a monopoly and does not have a trade barrier."
According to the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia, not only to allow the export of cashew nuts or price competition, but also to be ready to help farmers when prices fall to support the cultivation efforts of our Cambodian farmers. .
