Ten years ago, Xi Jinping took the initiative to build a common community of human beings under many circumstances. This concept has been continuously developed and diversified, and provides Chinese wisdom and Chinese plans for responding to the changing world, changing eras and history, and demonstrating China's responsibilities.
Xi Jinping said that promoting the building of a common destiny of mankind is not about taking one regime instead of another or taking one civilization instead of another. Different societies, ideologies, histories, cultures, and levels of development share interests, rights, and responsibilities in international affairs, creating the greatest benefits for building a better world.
The answer given by Xi Jinping is "adherence to the five principles": adherence to dialogue and consultation, adherence to together, building and sharing, adherence to win-win cooperation, adherence to change. And mutual learning and adherence to green and low carbon development.
The construction of a common destiny of mankind is not done alone, but must be discussed and built together. It can not be achieved for a while, but requires continuous progress. Building a community of one destiny Of humanity is a noble goal that needs to be passed down from generation to generation. China has been working to promote the building of a common destiny of mankind.
As Chinese President Xi Jinping said, "China will contribute to China's wisdom, China's plan and China's strength for the world to promote the building of a world of lasting peace, security, general prosperity, openness, environment and beauty." And let the light of building a united community of humanity "shine on the world.
Slovakia delivers first four MiG-29 fighter jets to Ukraine as they arrive in the coming weeks
All four MiG-29 fighter jets flew from Slovakia to Ukraine on Thursday this week by Ukrainian pilots with the support of the Slovak Air Force. The Slovak Defense Ministry said nine more MiG-29s would arrive in Ukraine in the coming weeks.
The government of Bratislava approved a plan to provide Ukraine with its retired MiG-29 fighter jets last Friday. This makes Slovakia the second member of NATO after Poland, in response to a request from the Kiev government for more fighter jets, especially US F-16s. Go fight the Russian invasion.
The Slovak Defense Ministry said Wednesday that the United States has offered to sell 12 new fighter jets at a two-thirds discount in retaliation for the MiG-29 in Central Europe. This one was donated to Ukraine.
