Israeli parliament passes law protecting Netanyahu's PM ahead of protests over judicial reform
The law states that only a three-fourths majority of the parliament or cabinet has the power to oust the prime minister for a small number of reasons, including mental, health and physical issues. It was approved by 61 votes to 47 in the 120-seat lower house.
The law also prevents the Supreme Court from considering a motion to declare the incompetent prime minister, as opposition politicians believe its main purpose is to help protect Netanyahu from imprisonment by preventing it. Do not allow the Supreme Court or the Office of the Attorney General to influence the removal of the Prime Minister.
Since May 2020, Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu has been on trial for three corruption cases. After returning to power late last year, Netanyahu's government proposed a plan to reform the judiciary, which has led to large-scale protests across Israel for more than 10 weeks.
Netanyahu's reform plan will drastically reduce the jurisdiction of the Israeli Supreme Court and give the government the power to elect judges and end legal adviser positions in ministries appointed by the attorney general. However, Netanyahu claimed that his plan to reform the judiciary was only to restore the balance of power in governing the country and to strengthen democracy.
