A new light of hope for children with special needs in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): A simple meeting with a student changed the life of 29-year-old Heng Sophanet forever. Sophanet used to work at one of the largest international schools in Phnom Penh. When Sophanet first left for work in 2016, her manager told her about some special children she would work with. She did not know much about this.
"My manager explained to me that one student had autism and the other had ADHD," says Sophanet. At the time, I did not understand the terms, so I went home and did some thorough research. I also learned that these are students with special needs that require the most love, care, patience and understanding.
Sophanet is the youngest and only Cambodian woman to receive a special education degree. She received her Master of Special Education from Flinders University in Australia in 2020. This is a source of pride that she has provided to the country and the people of Cambodia with this difficult but amazing achievement.
Educating and helping children with special needs is not easy, and it certainly brings with it many challenges. The most difficult situation is when parents are informed that their child has a disability and special needs. It is not only difficult for children but also for their parents.
I see a lot of positive factors in this area and since opening our Center for Special Needs and our website, I have seen a lot of progress. Now parents dare to talk about their children and also accept that their children have autism. I find that more and more people are accepting and trying to find out more about these conditions. "They are not as negative as in previous years."
Along with Sophanet's OrbRom special education center, she also works with children of all grades and trains teachers, and her next goal is to complete a doctorate in disability psychology. Sophanet, the first Cambodian to achieve so much at such a young age, must have worked hard to stand up for those children and to guide them on their beautiful path. .
