Prey Veng, Cambodia's largest rice producer, earns more than $ 1 billion in rice exports in five years
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Chea Somethy, Governor of Prey Veng Province, said that Prey Veng Province is the number one province in terms of rice production in Cambodia for 3 consecutive years, with an average production of more than 1 million tons per year, while 5 Last year, the province earned more than $ 1 billion from rice exports.
The statement of Prey Veng Provincial Governor was made at a press conference on "Five Years of Success of Prey Veng Provincial Administration" organized by the Spokesperson of the Royal Government at the Office of the Council of Ministers on March 22, 2023.
In the five years from 2018-2022, Prey Veng province produced a total of 7,465,194 tons of rice, after supplying the people in the province more than 5 million tons of rice for export, both domestically and abroad. About 1,250 million US dollars.
According to the Prey Veng Provincial Administration, Prey Veng Province, located in the plains and plains east of the Mekong River, is a province with high potential in agriculture, especially rice, with about 78% of households engaged in agriculture. Mainly. The province has a total cultivated area of 337,000 hectares, including 250,000 hectares of wet rice, 75,000 hectares of dry paddy and 12,000 hectares of mixed crops.
The total annual rice yield has been steadily increasing. The increase in the amount of rice is due to the technical support of rice production combined with the growth of irrigation, some places produce rice two to three times a year. In terms of gross income from rice production, the total production in the province is 1,866,298,500 US dollars.
Mr. Chea Somethy also said that the production of mixed crops has also made interesting progress, with the provincial administration establishing vegetable production communities to supply the provincial market, including the border provinces and Phnom Penh, which produce About 15 to 20 tons of vegetables are sold to the market per day.
Cambodia Prepares to Host the Third "Global Conference on Assistance to Mine Victims, Anti-Personnel and Explosive Remnants of War and the Rights of Persons with Disabilities" in October 2023
Senior Minister Ly Thuch stated that with the permission of Samdech Techo Prime Minister, Cambodia will be ready to host the "Global Conference on Assistance to Mine Victims, Anti-Personnel and Explosive Remnants of War and the Rights of Persons with Disabilities". Third, in October 2023.
The Mine Action Authority, which is staffed by the Royal Government in collaboration with ISU-APMBC and has financial support from the EU Global Project, is ready to share experiences on rescuing people with disabilities from landmines and explosive remnants of war and disseminate national policies. For the Protection of Persons with Disabilities, delegations from more than 40 countries will be invited to the Global Conference.
The third World Conference, hosted by Cambodia, will complement the results of two previous conferences held in Colombia and Jordan. The EU Global Plan will seek additional support from States Parties to the Convention on the Prohibition of Mine Action to carry out various treaty obligations, including victim assistance.
One of the aims of the Global Conference will be to provide input for new action plans under the Convention, providing a global forum for affected communities and survivors to raise issues related to victim assistance.
As Cambodia hosts the Global Conference, it will address the global challenges facing the implementation of mine and UXO relief efforts. Cambodia will focus on its efforts to rescue some of its victims, such as the inclusion of people with disabilities in sports.
The Global Conference is expected to bring together about 200 landmine victims and survivors, as well as rescue victims and people with disabilities / human rights, health, employment, development and other practitioners from 45 countries. And at least a quarter of participants will be from Cambodia, including from mine-affected areas.
