Kremlin: Putin to clarify Russia's position on Ukraine during Xi Jinping's visit
MOSCOW (Reuters) - Russian President Vladimir Putin will "explain in detail" Russia's stance on the conflict in Ukraine to Chinese President Xi Jinping during his state visit to Russia on Tuesday, the Kremlin said. This Monday, March 20. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Monday, March 20, 2023.
Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov said in a telephone interview that the two leaders would discuss issues in the peace plan for Ukraine proposed by Beijing last month.
"But here, of course, President Putin will provide a comprehensive explanation so that President Xi can understand the current situation directly from the Russian side," he stressed.
China has released its 12-point peace plan, calling for dialogue between the two sides but not including a roadmap for how to end the war. Due to China's refusal to condemn the Russian invasion, Ukraine was cautiously welcomed as the United States rejected it.
Chinese state media reported that Chinese President Xi Jinping arrived in Moscow on Monday evening for a three-day state visit.
Xi's current presence in Russia makes him the first Chinese president to shake hands with Putin since the International Criminal Court (ICC) issued an arrest warrant for him. From war crimes in Ukraine.
