The use of the national currency is crucial to strengthening monetary and political independence, maintaining macroeconomic stability and mitigating the effects of external crises.
(Phnom Penh): Ms. Chea Serey, Deputy Governor of the National Bank of Cambodia, continues to promote the use of the riel through images and other means.
She stressed that the use of the riel is important to strengthen monetary and political independence to contribute to maintaining macroeconomic stability and reducing the impact of external crises.
Ms. Chea Serey stated that the greater use of the riel will enable the National Bank of Cambodia to implement a more effective monetary and exchange rate policy, especially setting the base rate and providing additional cash to the economy, which could affect the exchange rate. Interest, marketing, investment and production will lead to job creation and increase people's income.
She noted that the experience of the Kovid-19 crisis has made it clear that the central banks of most monetaryly independent countries have been actively pursuing monetary policy, undermining monetary policy and lowering interest rates. In markets that have contributed to the rapid recovery of economic activity.
On the other hand, she said, the government can reduce its dependence on foreign debt by mobilizing local currency to support financing for infrastructure and development projects.
Dr. Sam Vichet, Senior Economist of the National Bank of Cambodia, confirmed that the current use of the riel is in good condition, in which savings in riel have increased significantly, so if there is an increase in salary payments in riel. Then we will get a lot of expenses and savings in riel.
He stressed that in order to promote the policy of using the riel to be linked to the policy of small and medium enterprises, when small and medium enterprises are well developed, the use of the riel will increase.
Stung Bat-Ban Nong Ian International Border Crossing Completed More Than 82% Completed in May
(Banteay Meanchey): Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, stated that as of March 2023, the project to prepare the Stung Bot-Ban Nong Ian International Border Gate, which is planned to use about 26.42 million US dollars, has been completed. About 82.30 percent of the construction is planned to be completed by the end of May 2023.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol stated this on the afternoon of March 20, 2023, on the occasion of leading a delegation from the Ministry to inspect the progress of the Stung Bot-Ban Nong Ian International Border Crossing Project with the participation of leaders. And many relevant professional officers.
Regarding the construction of the Stung Bot-Ban Nong Ian international border checkpoint, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol said, "Please be informed that as of March, he has done more than 82%, and the plan will end in May. "The construction company claims) but we came to see for ourselves and we realized that May may not be over, even with enough money, we do not think so."
With construction delays due to insufficient funds for the construction company, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol said the ministry would hold talks with the Thai side to speed up the process of lowering the budget for construction companies quickly. In order to be able to complete this project as planned, which will be completed in May.
According to Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, the main purpose of building the Stung Bot-Ban Nong Ian Bridge is to avoid traffic jams in Poipet, as all trucks are allowed to cross the bridge, and for tourists to continue through Poipet. .
Deputy Prime Minister Aun Porn Moniroth sets out 18 priorities to promote the implementation of the fourth phase of the Public Financial Management Reform Program
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Economy and Finance and Chairman of the Public Financial Management Reform Steering Committee, on the afternoon of March 20, 2023, attended the closing ceremony. To implement the fourth phase (2023-2027) "Achievement Accountability" of the Public Financial Management Reform Program.
On that occasion, the Deputy Prime Minister drew the attention of ministries-institutions, both national and sub-national, the General Secretariat of the Steering Committee for Public Financial Management Reform, the General Department of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, and All relevant units, on a number of practical activities that are of high priority under the Public Financial Management Reform Program - Phase 4, as follows:
Fourth, to promote the preparation of relevant regulations to ensure the implementation of the law on the public financial system and the law on public procurement (after the draft law on public procurement was approved and announced to be implemented in accordance with Procedure in force).
Fifth, to cooperate closely with the relevant ministries and institutions to promote and finalize the amendment of the Law on the Financial Regime and the Management of State Assets of the Sub-National Administration in accordance with the Law on the Public Financial System 2023.
Sixth, promote the full automation of the implementation of the budget according to FMIS and ensure the efficiency of the use of FMIS optimally. Both within the framework of ministries and professional institutions, in order to "implement the basics" of the achievement information budget from 2026 onwards.
Eighth, the Ministry of Economy and Finance and the specialized ministries and institutions must continue to pay more attention to improving the quality, content and structure of the program, as well as the administrative structure; As well as defining clear lines of accountability at each level of the program and administrative structure.
Tenth: Promote the strengthening of the internal control system to be effective by giving more authority to the practitioners, in which the management or management must be responsible for the achievement or control of the results. In practice, the part of financial responsibility is to strengthen the function of financial control in the relevant ministries and institutions, the function of internal audit and the function of financial inspector.
Eleventh: Continue to promote the preparation and implementation of achievement agreements between the Ministry of Economy and Finance and specialized ministries and institutions. In this regard, the General Department of Budget of the Ministry of Economy and Finance shall continue to support and provide additional training on the preparation of achievement agreements, as well as regular monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of achievement agreements.
Twelfth: The General Secretariat of the Public Financial Management Reform Steering Committee shall promote the finalization of the guidelines on public financial management of the Government and of the Ministries and Institutions and the Joint Framework, Roadmap and Action Plan for Settlement. Expenditure Arrears to increase efficiency and effectiveness in managing and implementing budget expenditures.
13th: Continue to pay attention to strengthening the capacity of public financial management, both within the Ministry of Economy and Finance, both in ministries and institutions, and in sub-national administrations, in order to support the reform work. And to increase the efficiency of daily activities.
14th: Continue to pay more attention to the management of state property through the implementation of information technology management system, state property inventory (also known as SARMIS) and finalization of the master plan for management. State property.
16th: Continue to cooperate with relevant development partners on a number of initiatives within the framework of the Public Financial Management Reform Program, including: green public financial management, gender mainstreaming in the budget, and debt diversification. Public, especially through the development of state securities.
17th: Promote the implementation of a detailed action plan and monitoring and monitoring mechanism for the implementation of the document "Vision and Strategy on the harmonization of 3 + 1 reforms", including: National program for democratic development at the sub-national level; Public Financial Management Reform Program; National Program for Public Administration Reform and Law and Judicial Reform Program.
