Health Science Students from Cambodian Muslim Health Science Students Association Visit Samdech Mey Library
(Phnom Penh): On the afternoon of March 19, 2023, a group of 50 Cambodian Muslim medical students led by Mr. San Sap E, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health and President of the Cambodian Muslim Health Science Students Association, visited the Samdech Mey Nei Library. DC-Cam with a warm welcome from Mr. Youk Chhang, Director of the Documentation Center of Cambodia.
On that occasion, Mr. Youk Chhang said that there are 5 million people who survived the Khmer Rouge regime, which is equivalent to 33% of the population throughout Cambodia, which does not seem to receive much attention. These survivors rebuilt the country from the bare hands of our aunts and uncles. They are now getting older and starting to get sick.
(Phnom Penh): The Documentation Center of Cambodia project "Promoting the Rights and Improving the Health of Khmer Rouge Survivors" organized a one-day workshop on "Health Education" on March 19, 2023, yesterday at the Cambodiana Hotel with 50 health science students from the Cambodian Muslim Health Science Students Association and 2 public health scholarship students of the Documentation Center of Cambodia.
This project aims to provide better access to health services for survivors of the Khmer Rouge regime and to have the participation of a group of student volunteers who are descendants of those survivors of the Khmer Rouge regime. Volunteer students can return to work in their communities.
Ms. Sofarina also presented a map of a documentary of 1,030 Cambodian volunteers from the Documentation Center of Cambodia interviewing 32,000 Khmer Rouge survivors living in remote rural areas of Cambodia and a private clinic at the Documentation Center. Cambodia has worked with the past.
Later, Dr. Liza Brown, a professor at Stanford and Palauto University, emphasized the importance of treating the Khmer Rouge survivors of mental illness: The Khmer Rouge has experienced it in the past. The mental illness came shortly after the Khmer Rouge survivors and some of the mental illness lasted for a long time.
Some survivors have tried to hide and escape mental illness without treatment, but the disease will return as they get older and live in isolation. In addition, the patient must acknowledge that he or she has a mental illness in order for him or her to be prepared to consult a specialist.
Separately, Dr. Kouch Mengly, President of Mengly J Foundation. Kouch said that health and health care are very important for the people of Cambodia. However, he said that these problems are also related to the understanding of the doctors who provide health services. Obviously, the doctor must have enough time to advise the patient to know the initial cause of the disease and to use various methods to treat.
Mr. San Sap Ey, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health and President of the Cambodian Muslim Health Science Students Association, emphasized that learning health sciences is not easy. When we take this course, we have to ask ourselves what we study for.
"If we learn to save people, we will strengthen the capacity and good ethics of patients," he said. We will see the value of work and be proud of ourselves. He instructed all students to pay attention, listen to the workshops and participate actively and apply the knowledge gained in their work.
In Islam, there are only three things that we can take with us:
1: Worship of Islam 2: Donation 3: Sharing knowledge to Muslims and the general public
Finally, he instructed all students to cultivate all the ideas from school and be ready when they graduate and go to work in the hospital.
He first traveled to remote rural areas of Cambodia with DC-Cam staff to interview Khmer Rouge survivors who had lodged complaints with the tribunal about health issues, and then worked on the project. Promoting the rights and improving the health status of Khmer Rouge survivors ”to help Khmer Rouge survivors 50 years of age and older.
However, the horrors left behind by the Khmer Rouge regime have persisted for many years, so these stories should not be forgotten as the atrocities continue to age, especially in the rural areas. Remote in Cambodia. More than 40 years have passed and now the voices of Khmer Rouge survivors are still being heard.
The wounds and bad memories of the survivors of the Khmer Rouge regime that lasted for many years and did not leave them. Almost all survivors of the Khmer Rouge lived in poverty and suffered from age-related illnesses, combined with a lack of awareness of health care and a lack of access to clinics and hospitals.
DC-Cam has been doing this since the beginning of 2016, and a questionnaire was developed to collect personal data and health issues based on common disease symptoms and psychological impacts on the health of Khmer Rouge survivors. . After more than a year, almost 95% of the 1,750 survivors of the Khmer Rouge living in seven provinces were interviewed for medical examinations and treatment under the guidance of a doctor.
Responses from Khmer Rouge survivors were collected, evaluated and analyzed, and the results are as follows:
