(Phnom Penh): "Krama" or "Khmer traditional towel" is a national identity and an indispensable object in the daily life of the Cambodian people from birth to death, and today the krama is still used. Always. In Cambodian society, the krama is widely used at all levels, from ordinary people to top leaders at all times, on special occasions and everywhere.
In addition to the general use, the krama is also used for rituals or ceremonies to participate in national and religious ceremonies, used as an offering or offering in ceremonies, used as a gift to elders or relatives, used as a bond and souvenir between couples or A couple and so on.
Cambodia is 80% done to prepare documents for the UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity list, and if nothing changes, Cambodia will apply on March 31, 2023. This is according to an official of the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts told Fresh News.
According to Mr. Rath Sandab, an expert on Khmer civilization, he explained the meaning of the krama thread, saying that weaving one thread at a time signifies unity in the same society. The interconnection of cells from one fiber to another until we can use this is very valuable in the Khmer civilization to connect.
He claims that according to the inscriptions, the use of the krama dates back to pre-Christian times, that is, 200 years before Christ. The use of this krama is both traditional and religious, as evidenced by the ceremonial and ceremonial attire of the grandmothers during festivals and pagodas.
"The meaning of the krama in the Khmer civilization is a connection in our traditions, showing the greatness of our ancestral heritage that we know how to use and can be used as a body covering," said Rath. This is a great value for our Khmer krama.
He expressed his happiness that officials, civil servants and people at all levels are still active in using the krama. Regarding the request for a krama to be inscribed on the UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity list, he considered it to be an expression of the value of Khmer clothing and to identify the Khmer national identity, and this proposal also shows the preservation of Khmer traditions on dress. And Khmer domination as well.
Dr. Kin Phea is a Cambodian teacher and observer who has been observed to use the krama regularly, whether in small or large programs, to teach or to meet with national and international guests. Like going abroad. For him, the krama is a beautiful ornament and a display of national identity to others.
Not only for personal use, Dr. also used the krama as a souvenir for friends. He recalled that he had provided scholars from 18 countries during his participation in the US SUSI Program, which ran from June 21 to March 3. August 2014 in New York, Philadelphia and Washington, DC.
Regarding the request for the krama to be inscribed on the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity list, Dr. Kin Phea thinks it is a good thing because it shows that we care about the cultural heritage of our ancestors and that the krama is our Khmer identity. If we put it into heritage, it is our pride that shows that we are very rich in cultural heritage.
He said that in the past, we did not pay much attention because our country was at war. Now we have peace, we are rich, and our Royal Government of Cambodia, with the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts as its staff, we see that he is working hard to preserve our Khmer cultural heritage, both tangible and intangible.
He inspired Cambodians to use more krama, especially in official programs. He added that if at the Royal Academy of Cambodia, if there are visitors, we always give them a krama as a souvenir.
Many other Cambodian people who use Khmer krama are proud that this traditional object is planned to be inscribed on the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity list. Fresh News continued to interview Mr. Nuon Phearum, a resident of Svay Rieng province, and Mr. Em Sophal, Vice President of the Reachsey Association in Phnom Penh.
"As we all know, through the initiative of the Union of Youth Federations of Cambodia and the participation of the Cambodian family, the longest krama record in the world has been broken and recognized by the Commission. The world record on July 1, 2018. This is according to Mr. Nuon Phearum.
Mr. Nuon Phearum believes that the inclusion of the Krama in the UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity list in the near future is indeed worthy of enhancing Cambodia's prestige on the international stage and promoting the Khmer cultural heritage. Belonging to Cambodia for the world to know and as a treasure to be protected, as well as cultural property, both tangible and intangible, which have been registered in the past.
"On my own behalf, as well as the entire Reachsei Association, I am very proud of the Khmer krama, which is being inscribed on the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity list, because of the fact that the use of the krama has many benefits and "There is an easy form of our Cambodian people in their daily lives that this image of the krama has emerged as a cultural identity of the Khmer nation."
According to Mr. Em Sophal, it is a special point that he is very interested and saddened that the beautiful Khmer krama was changed, the meaning scared the Cambodian people during the dark period of Democratic Kampuchea by just seeing you Paying the krama is understood as an organizational person. But now it is the pride of our Khmer krama that has re-established the fine cultural characteristics of the Khmer people.
Officials at the Ministry of Culture recalled the story of Cambodia breaking the record for the longest krama in the world in 2018. He claimed that the work to apply for the inscription on the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity has been underway for two years.
