20-year-old dolphin found dead in Stung Treng province
(Phnom Penh): A 20-year-old dolphin was found dead at Koh Kon Sat Dolphin Conservation Cave, Koh Preah Commune, Siem Bok District, Stung Treng Province at 6:30 am on March 19, 2023.
According to experts from the Fisheries Administration and WWF, the dead dolphin, ID number 056, weighs 160 kilograms, is 2.38 meters long and is about 20 years old. Experts suspect that the dolphin died from a fishing net because there were scars on the abdomen, nets at the base of the tail and side fins.
This is the first dolphin to die in 2023. Mekong River dolphins are a living natural treasure with about 90 species living along the Mekong River in Kratie and Stung Treng provinces. Adult dolphins can reproduce from the age of seven and give birth every two years.
Currently, dolphins are threatened by fishing gear, nets and shocks. Therefore, the Royal Government issued Sub-Decree No. 47, dated 27 February 2023, defining the 120 km long dolphin protection and conservation area from the area under the Cambodia-China Friendship Bridge, Stung Treng, Stung Treng Province to the head of Koh Krom. Kratie Province by banning fishing with fishing nets and other illegal fishing gear.
Meanwhile, the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries has also cooperated with the Kratie and Stung Treng provincial administrations and relevant partner organizations to continue to strengthen the protection of dolphin habitat, as well as educate local people about the value of dolphins. This invaluable globalization of Mekong dolphins to contribute to the sustainability of Mekong dolphins and natural fisheries resources.
The Fisheries Administration urges fishermen in the area to immediately stop using nets, fishing nets and other illegal fishing gear in the dolphin area to ensure the sustainability of our dolphins and breeding grounds for tourism. The reputation of the nation will be known and preserved for future generations.
