The flames of war in Cambodia in the past have caused many Cambodian artifacts, statues and jewels to be stolen, sold, and dumped around the world. According to Hab Touch, these treasures were lost during the National War, especially during the 1970s.
But as Cambodia regained its peace, Cambodian artifacts were constantly sought and reclaimed. Mr. Hap Touch claimed that the artifacts that Cambodia found and demanded to return were hundreds. Countries where Cambodia has found and reclaimed these artifacts include the United States, the United Kingdom, and a total of 15 other countries.
According to Hab Touch, getting these artifacts back is not an easy task, with the toughest compromises and advocacy.
"Our job is not just to demand, it means that our first choice is to negotiate how to get it back to us," said Hap Touch. We present evidence, we negotiate. "If negotiations do not move forward, we will continue to cooperate with the United States and the United Kingdom or other countries to provide evidence and seize or go to court if necessary."
The Secretary of State claimed that Cambodia had become one of the leading countries in launching operations to reclaim Cambodian cultural property that was being traded back.
Asked what was the key to Cambodia's efforts to find and reclaim Cambodian treasures, Hap Touch claimed that these treasures were the soul and identity of the Cambodian nation that needed to be reclaimed.
"This is the ancestral soul, this is the identity of a nation. If we lose all that, we lose our identity. If one loses one's identity, one has only a shell, no soul. That is why we are working so hard for the Cambodian people now and in the future. ” This is the confirmation of Mr. Hap Touch.
For Hap Touch, Cambodia's ability to find and reclaim artifacts also depends on a peaceful Cambodia. He stressed that peace is a golden opportunity for Cambodia to find the soul of the Cambodian nation back.
