The 27-nation European Union is Thailand's fourth largest trading partner after China, the United States and Japan.
The FTA negotiations began in 2023 but were suspended after the 2014 coup when the European Union protested what it called a suspension of democracy. After Thailand's new constitution was approved in 2017 and elections held in 2019, relations between the two sides improved, leading to the signing of a partnership and cooperation agreement in December. Before.
By 2022, trade between Thailand and the EU will reach 41 billion US dollars, accounting for 7% of Thailand's total trade.
