The February 28 tragedy revealed decades of security failures on Greek railways and put tremendous pressure on conservative governments ahead of national elections.
The 24-hour strike on Thursday this week, the largest number in days since the disaster, involved major Greek private and public unions.
Last month, a passenger train and a freight train collided head-on, killing 57 people. It is the deadliest train accident Greece has ever experienced in about 50 years.
