(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports instructs both public and private educational institutions to bring students to play games and Khmer folk dances in preparation for the celebration and the traditional New Year celebration. At the same time, the Ministry forbids playing with water spray and spraying powder.
The Ministry emphasized that bringing students to play games and folk dances to participate in the preservation and promotion of culture, such as beliefs, customs, traditions of the Khmer nation to survive and grow, as well as to avoid other dangers.
In this instruction, the Ministry has instructed educational institutions to create conditions for children, students and youth to play games and Khmer folk dances before and on the occasion of the traditional New Year, such as ballroom dancing, Saravan dance, trout dance, etc. And some Khmer folk games, such as throwing a box, throwing a box, grabbing a leaf, and so on.
The Ministry also advises teachers, children's councils, and youth councils to encourage and facilitate young people, students and youth to play games and Khmer folk dances, as in the first point, effectively and safely.
At the same time, the Ministry has instructed educational institutions to prohibit and prevent any games that are not games or Khmer folk dances and some games that face unsafe and dangerous risks, such as burning or firing fireworks, spraying water, spraying paint, etc.
The Ministry also recommends that educational institutions work closely with parents or guardians of students, communities, and local authorities to provide and receive information on unsafe gambling, both inside and outside the educational institution, in order to jointly prevent and solve problems in a timely manner. Mr. Hun Many told all forces to continue to believe in the policy set by the Royal Government
