Russia's new law will automatically revoke the citizenship of individuals critical of the war in Ukraine (Video inside) (Moscow): Russian media reported on Wednesday that a proposed amendment to the Russian citizenship law would allow for the revocation of citizenship under charges. Accused of treason and defamation of its military operations in Ukraine. This is according to the publication by the AFP news agency on Wednesday, March 15, 2023.
The Kremlin claims that according to opinion polls, most Russians support its actions in Ukraine. However, hundreds of thousands of people who do not agree with the decision or are afraid to go to war have fled the country and those who refuse are considered to be defaming the army. Defamation of the military is punishable by up to five years in prison, while intentional dissemination of false information is punishable by up to 15 years in prison.
The CIS was established in 1991 by Russia and a group of countries of the former Soviet republics. According to data from the Russian Ministry of Internal Affairs, in 2022, more than 691,000 people received Russian citizenship, almost half of them from Eurasian countries.
