Dr. Meak Bora, President of the National Language Institute of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, describes the "evolution of the Khmer language and script"
(Phnom Penh): Language, as well as an element of civilization, always changes, grows or declines and dies in accordance with the life of human society and each nation. Thus, according to research, we see that the Khmer language has evolved into three major stages: 1. The ancient Khmer language from the time of the first Khmer people until the 15th century AD.
In this period, the Khmer language was complicated and faced many crises due to the war of aggression from neighboring countries and the civil war, which caused the language and spelling of the Khmer language to deteriorate drastically, the learning lacked unity. Learn at the pagoda and the respective areas, especially in writing or writing manuscripts, there are different writing, some words.
By the time of the French, the Khmer language had lost its prestige to another level, with the French forcing them to learn their language as a vehicle. In addition, the French wanted to change the Khmer script to the Latin script. However, the Khmer language and script have not lost their prestige due to the efforts of the monks in the pagodas, and the current Khmer language dates from the late 19th century to the present.
Based on the above two evidences, the expression "Khmer can do" is not misspelled. As a researcher of the Khmer language and script, I would like to commend all the people who are sick of the national language and script of the nation, with concern and lack of agreement on the spelling.
By: Dr. Meak Bora, President of the National Language Institute of the Royal Academy of Cambodia
