China, Thailand sign memorandum on translation of ancient books
BEIJING: China's National Administration of Press and Publishing on Monday signed a memorandum of understanding with the Thai Ministry of Higher Education, Science, Research and Innovation on the translation and publication of ancient works from both countries. This is according to the release by the Xinhua news agency on Tuesday, March 14, 2023.
According to the memorandum, 50 ancient works from these countries will be translated and published over the next five years in an effort to promote mutual understanding and friendship between the Chinese and Thai peoples.
The signing of the MoU is a historic achievement in exchanging and promoting Sino-Thai cooperation, and it is expected to create new opportunities and be a driving force for people-to-people exchanges. People and cultural exchanges and mutual learning between the two countries.
Japan Provides Over $ 200,000 Grant for Bridge Construction and Strengthening of Cambodian Chicken Chickens
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Japan has agreed to provide a total of US $ 209,197 to two recipient institutions under the Grants for Small-Scale Human Security Project (Kusanoni Grant) for bridge construction and value chain strengthening. Khmer farm chicken.
The grant agreement was signed on March 14, 2023 by Mr. Uno Atsushi, Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary of Japan to the Kingdom of Cambodia and representatives of both recipients.
The two projects include:
First: Bridge construction project in Koul commune, Prasat Sambo district, Kampong Thom province (US $ 100,394) Recipient: Department of Public Works and Transport of Kampong Thom
Second: Project to strengthen the value chain of Khmer chicken production in two provinces of Cambodia (budget 108,803 USD) Recipient: Heifer International Cambodia
In this project, chicken raising equipment will be installed and repaired for 17 chicken farmers in Kampong Chhnang and Siem Reap provinces. These direct beneficiaries will also receive technical assistance from Heifer International Cambodia and the farming community. The skills and knowledge gained will be shared with other members of the farming community, and part of the proceeds from the project will be used to build more poultry facilities.
As a result, a total of 56 members of the farming community in Kampong Chhnang and 227 in Siem Reap will gain knowledge on safe and effective chicken raising techniques, hygiene practices and management skills. By strengthening the value chain of chicken production, we expect farmers' incomes to increase.
Japan's Kusano grant began in Cambodia in 1991 to support Cambodia's efforts to develop and rebuild the country at the grassroots level. The grant aims to protect vulnerable people from a number of factors, such as poverty and / or misfortune, which could directly threaten their lives, livelihoods and dignity, as well as promote their self-reliance. Communities.
Since 1991, the Government of Japan has provided more than $ 66 million to local authorities and NGOs to implement 705 Kusano Projects throughout Cambodia.
