In fact, fresh banana exports in January were more than 20,000 tons, and in February were nearly 60,000 tons, an increase due to declining transportation combined with China's economic recovery.
"The export of fresh bananas for 2023 has increased compared to the same period in 2022," said Duke Hun Lak, a well-known Cambodian banana exporter. "In January, the export of fresh bananas to China was 28941 tons, while in February was 59,199 tons, which is an increase compared to the same period in 2022."
The tycoon stated that the factor that caused the increase in exports this year was because this year, China, the main market of Cambodia, has reopened the country, and the traffic flow in the market has resumed, which is the reason for the increase in exports. Banana production increased, on the other hand, earlier this year, the cost of transportation has been steadily declining, almost equal to normal prices, these factors have led to an increase in fresh banana exports.
Oknha Lim Heng, vice president of the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce, told Fresh News on March 14 that most of these goods were exported to the United States and the European Union. The increase in exports of this type of goods reflects the increase in investment in Cambodia's light industry as Cambodia introduces new laws that encourage investment in the electronics sector, which attracts investors to Cambodia to produce and process. .
He said that the increase in exports of electronic equipment as well as electricity, components and parts is a good sign for the processing and assembly industry in Cambodia.
According to the roadmap for the development of the automotive and electronics sector in Cambodia, which was recently released by the Royal Government, the Royal Government aims to increase the export volume in the electronics sector to 1,600 million US dollars and create more than 10,000 new jobs. 2027.
According to the roadmap, exports of electronics in the last 10 years increased six times to about 923 million US dollars in 2019, while the number of qualified investment projects increased to 89 projects in 2019. .
Japan and China are the main sources of investment in the electronics sector in Cambodia, while the main export markets are the United States, ASEAN, China, Japan and South Korea. Increased investment in the sector has boosted the workforce by as much as 70 times in the last 10 years, with the current workforce of about 30,000.
According to the roadmap, "the growing demand for electronics around the world has been giving Cambodia more opportunities to capture market share of other high-demand products."
