The Environmental Education Working Group will host performances on three main topics: linking forests and rainfall, generating revenue through the sale of forest carbon credits, and achieving zero wildlife traps. These events are organized by the Wildlife Alliance in collaboration with its close partner Baseet, an educational institution focused on science, technology, engineering and mathematics.
Dr. Su Vanna Gonlet, President of the Wildlife Alliance (WA), said: “More than 20 years ago, the Ministry of Environment and the Wildlife Alliance began joint patrols in the Cardamom Mountains. From the beginning, the villagers have always relied on the forest for their livelihood. ”
"But today, thanks to the latest advances in climate science, we are all aware of the benefits of forest protection, including the financial benefits of supporting community livelihoods," said Dr. Sou Vanna Gonlet.
These financial benefits were generated by the REDD + South Cardamom Mountains project, which was established in 2015 and has since generated revenue from the sale of forest carbon credits. The Red Plus South Cardamom Range project is led by the Ministry of Environment and implemented by the Wildlife Alliance.
According to Sok Kheng Vin, Undersecretary of State at the Ministry of Environment, the purpose of the project is "The purpose of the REDD + South Cardamom Range is to reduce climate change through forest conservation, which is a water regulator. Rain around the world. "This project has created livelihood opportunities for local communities."
The Cardamom Mountains are the focal point of biodiversity, the last remaining elephant corridor in Asia, and home to 52 endangered wildlife species listed on the I-Red List. UCN These are the reasons why the carbon credit in the REDD + Plus Cardamom Range project is financially viable and attracts customers from all over the world.
By rehabilitating the big tigers in the Cardamom Forest, it will be able to enhance the growth of biodiversity and make the project more attractive to customers. The Big Tiger Rehabilitation Project is a joint project between the Royal Government of Cambodia and the Government of India based on the recent signing of a Memorandum of Understanding between the two countries, a historic step forward for the conservation of the Big Tiger.
