Japan joins new trade dispute settlement mechanism set up on behalf of WTO (Video inside) (Geneva): Japan joins a new trade dispute settlement mechanism Which was set up temporarily to replace the World Trade Organization (WTO) dispute settlement body. According to Reuters.
Japan's involvement with the Multilateral Appellate Arbitration Court (MPIA) is seen by observers as encouraging more countries to join.
The WTO's High-Level Trade Dispute Settlement Body has been stalled for more than two years due to a stalemate over the appointment of a new judge during the administration of former President Donald Trump. The United States, which continues to oppose calls for the immediate appointment of judges, is instead leading discussions on how to reform the dispute resolution body.
According to the MPIA website, Japan, a regular user of the WTO dispute settlement system, including in recent cases with South Korea, is the 26th member of the MPIA Appeal Arbitration Unit. Other parties include the European Union, Canada and Brazil. According to Reuters.
