Understand Business: Experts Share Tips That Cambodian SMEs Should Strengthen to Do Business with Japan
Mr. Khim Leang, Vice President of the Cambodia-Japan Trade and Investment Association (CJBI), said that after the 19-Kovid disease was alleviated, many Japanese investors came to Cambodia and contacted CJBI to explore investment opportunities. One for entrepreneurs and SME owners to collaborate with those investors to expand their business and help them run their business better.
He sees that Cambodian investors are also looking for business partners from Japan, but nevertheless, in order to partner with Japanese investors, Cambodian entrepreneurs need to strengthen their own capabilities first, especially in terms of business management, value, accountability. Original.
He said: "What I know from the past on the Japanese side that he demanded from the Cambodian side, we are related to the ability to manage the business, in other words, internal management, such as income and expenditure list, finance, staff. In particular, the management of documents to be clear.
Mr. Taing Nguon Ly, Executive Director of the new "Techo" Business Training Center, urges SMEs in Cambodia to transition from a family business to a properly registered corporation with access to accounting systems and access to loans. Make their business stronger and more attractive to foreign investment partners.
He encourages all business owners to register if they want to find a partner, especially investors from Japan, because if they do not register the business properly, investors will not consider cooperating with.
"We know they may not be able to register immediately, but we want to strengthen and try to transition from a family business to a more competitive form of business using account management procedures properly," he said. Personnel management. "It is very important to first attract investment to his investment and be able to get loans from other banks to expand his business."
He observed that at present, Cambodian SMEs have not reached a level that can compete because they have not modernized the production of goods are still expensive and the packaging is not good.
According to the latest survey by the Ministry of Planning, there are currently about 750,000 small and medium enterprises in Cambodia, but only 12% of them are registered. This is according to Mr. Taing Nguon Ly.
Dr. Chheang Vann Monin, Executive Director of Cambodian Entrepreneurship, said that if Cambodian SMEs can cooperate with foreign partners, it is a good opportunity for local business owners, which makes them more able to export goods abroad.
He added that the SME that can attract cooperation with Japanese investors is the business owner who worked in Japan and returned home because they understand the needs of the Japanese people and have an existing network that can produce and export to this country. To meet the requirements.
