Reuters quotes Albanese as saying in a speech to the AFR Business Summit in Sydney that Australia wants "greater diversification with the partners we trade with and greater diversity in the country." "In what we trade, which means our economy is more resilient and secure."
China is Australia's largest trading partner, although diplomatic disputes have caused "trade barriers" imposed on Australian exports.
Canberra has called on Beijing to lift the embargo imposed on Australian exports as the two countries resume talks after a stalemate in diplomatic talks.
Business leaders across the transport, financial resources, higher education, architecture and energy sectors will on Wednesday accompany the Albanese prime minister to India, Australia's sixth largest trading partner.
Australia will supply essential minerals to India while producing electric vehicle parts such as batteries and other green energy products in Australia, according to Reuters, Albanese said in the speech.
"We can do all this work, as well as the rest of the work, to be a reliable and trustworthy energy supplier to major trading partners such as Japan and the Republic of Korea," he said.
Authorities signed a letter of intent for the IMF loan on Monday, the president said.
China's support completes the support Sri Lanka needs from creditor countries to pave the way for the IMF board to approve the loan, which was approved by the fund's staff working group in September.
The bailout talks have dragged on for months as Beijing has demanded that multilateral institutions such as the World Bank join in debt restructuring for indebted countries, which has escalated tensions with the United States.
The bailout will pave the way for more funding for Sri Lanka and set debt restructuring for the bankrupt country on a more stable path since bankruptcy last year. According to Bloomberg News.
CSX agrees to allow two companies to list and sell equity and bonds
In order to be officially listed and traded on the Cambodia Securities Exchange, both companies must obtain a public offering from the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia (NCCC) and must meet certain other requirements regarding listing. Listed on the Cambodia Securities Exchange.
Mr. Hong Sohour, the Royal Government Delegate in charge of the CEO of the Cambodia Securities Exchange, said that once the two companies meet the legal requirements, they will receive permission to issue securities from the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia. The securities market is expected to sell during April and May.
He added that in addition to the above two companies, at the beginning of 2023, the Cambodia Securities Exchange has received three companies that step into the stock exchange as a great motivation for the efforts to continue to develop the securities sector to be more useful for. Business owners and for the Cambodian national economy.
Mr. Hong Sohour added that next week, the Cambodia Securities Exchange will examine the possibility of applying for listing of another large company.
Court Sentences 4 Thieves Who Seize Foreigners' Phones in Russey Keo District
(Phnom Penh): Ms. Say Srey Leak, President of the Council of Judges of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court, on the morning of March 9, 2023, announced the verdict in the criminal case of 4 defendants involved in the case of mobile phone seizure. Held at the point of Street 105, Group 3, Bak Touk Village, Sangkat Toul Sangke I, Khan Russey Keo, Phnom Penh, during October 2022.
Judge Say Sreyleak dropped the charges against one of the accused and sentenced three defendants to one year, six months and three years in prison each. Charge them from a crime to a misdemeanor.
These criminals are difficult to prevent and crack down on the authorities, and this is also a corner of the protection of security and public order: the authorities have tried to crack down on crime to protect national and international visitors and attract domestic tourism and national honor to cause. Up by this group in the past.
When the verdict was announced, Judge Say Sreyleak stated that the four accused were: 17-year-old Ly Al-Rifin alias Ron Arifin, sentenced to 1 year and 6 months in prison; Year 3: 18-year-old Duong Darasak was sentenced to three years in prison each, and 23-year-old Man Hafisin was acquitted.
All four were arrested on October 31, 2022. After their arrest, Russey Keo district police confiscated exhibits, including two motorcycles belonging to the suspect and a mobile phone.
